Article 14

1. The Regional Government is obliged and responsible to implement efforts to protect women and children victims of violence through:  a. policy determination;
b. preparation of program and activity planning;
c. establishment of P2TP2A;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure;
e. allocating budget; and  f. establishment of FPKK.
2. To anticipate the occurrence of acts of violence, the Regional Government organizes services for victims in the form of:  a. facilitating the establishment of P2TP2A;
b. facilitating P2TP2A facilities and infrastructure according to capacity; and  c. facilitating FPKK as a network forum for victim handling.

Article 16

The community and family have obligations and responsibilities which include:
a. preventing the occurrence of violence against women and children;
11 -
b. reporting if violence occurs;
c. protecting victims; and  d. providing emergency assistance.

Article 19

1. The organizational structure of P2TP2A established by the Regional Government as referred to in Article 17 paragraph (1) consists of:  a. general chairperson or other equivalent designation;
b. deputy general chairperson;
c. executive chairperson;
d. deputy executive chairperson;
e. secretary;
f. treasurer; and  g. executive chairperson overseeing fields.
2. Further provisions regarding the structure and duties of P2TP2A established by the Region as referred to in paragraph (1) are stipulated by Mayor's decree.

12 -

Article 20

1. P2TP2A established by the Regional Government must have at least three counselors.
2. Counselors as referred to in paragraph (1) include:  a. counselor in the field of law;
b. counselor in the field of health; and  c. counselor in the field of psychology.
3. The implementation of counseling carried out by counselors can be implemented in a networked manner as follows:  a. counseling in the field of law by involving the police, prosecutor's office, district court, religious court, legal advocacy institutions and Non-Governmental Organizations;
b. counseling in the field of health by involving the Health Office, Regional General Hospital and other health services;
c. counseling in the field of psychology by involving psychological companions and/or psychologists.

Article 21

1. Efforts to prevent violence against women and children are carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the regional apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented by:  a. forming a work network in efforts to prevent violence;
b. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;

13 -
c. forming a violence prevention system;
d. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence; and  e. providing critical education on the rights of women and children for the community.

Article 22

In addition to the Regional Government, prevention efforts as referred to in Article 21 paragraph (1) are carried out by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. educational institutions; and  d. Non-Governmental Organizations.

Article 24

The implementation of services to victims is carried out with:  a. speed;
b. safety and comfort;
c. a sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easy accessibility;
f. no charges; and  g. guaranteed confidentiality.

14 -

Article 25

Forms of services to victims include:
a. complaint services, consultation, and counseling;
b. assistance services;
c. health services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services; and  f. repatriation and social reintegration services.

Article 27

Assistance services as referred to in Article 25 letter b include:
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;
b. accompanying victims during the medicolegal process;
c. accompanying victims during the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and court;
d. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;
e. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including coverage by mass media;
f. conducting coordination with other companions; and  g. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

